5.4 Complaint status system

Statuses (simplify only if the existing implementation genuinely needs fewer, and then keep all essential workflow information):

submitted → under_review → accepted → in_progress → resolved and rejected. Withdrawal is represented by withdrawn: true together with a history entry (status may stay as-is or become withdrawn; choose one approach, document it, and apply it consistently).

Valid transitions (enforce on the server and, where possible, in Firestore rules):

From	To (allowed)	Who
submitted	under_review	Admin
submitted	rejected	Admin (reason required)
under_review	accepted, rejected	Admin (rejection reason required)
under_review	under_review with "clarification requested"	Admin (see below)
accepted	in_progress	Admin
in_progress	resolved	Admin (resolution summary required; evidence photo strongly encouraged)
submitted, under_review, accepted	withdrawn	Citizen (owner only)
in_progress	withdrawn	Decide and document (default: not allowed)
resolved, rejected	withdrawn	Not allowed

Clarification requests: an admin may request clarification before accepting. Implement it as a status-history entry plus a clarificationRequest message visible to the citizen (and a way for the citizen to reply with a text note), without adding a new top-level status unless the code already has one.

Every status change records: previous status, new status, timestamp, actor (admin UID or "system"), and explanation. Invalid transitions are rejected with a clear error. Citizens and admins must always see the same status for the same report (single source of truth: the status field).

5.5 AI civic image and report analysis

AI analysis is a core feature and it must be honest.

One integrated action

Provide a single button: Analyze with Civic AI. It analyses the uploaded image and the description together. Remove separate buttons such as "Suggest Category with AI", "Check with Civic AI" and "Verify Image with AI".

Inputs

Only the uploaded photograph and the citizen's description. Do not send Google Maps or location data to Gemini for category or severity decisions.

Required structured output

Make Gemini return strict JSON matching a schema (use Gemini structured output / responseSchema with responseMimeType: "application/json" if the existing model supports it). Validate the parsed JSON on the server with a schema validator (for example Zod). Reject or retry once if invalid.

json
{
  "suggestedCategory": "pothole",
  "imageRelevance": "civic | unclear | unrelated",
  "visibleObservations": ["only things directly visible in the image"],
  "inferences": ["things the AI is assuming; each clearly an assumption"],
  "needsHumanVerification": ["things that cannot be established from the photo"],
  "suggestedSeverity": "low | medium | high",
  "severityReasoning": "short explanation grounded in visible evidence",
  "explanation": "plain-language summary",
  "municipalRecommendations": ["what a department could inspect or do"],
  "citizenGuidance": ["safe actions, what to avoid, how to document"],
  "limitations": ["e.g. photo is dark, scale unknown"],
  "descriptionMatchesImage": true
}

The server (not the model) derives the final fields shown to users: aiVerificationStatus and confidenceLabel.

Verification status (server-derived)
verified: image is civic-relevant and the description is consistent with it.
unclear: image quality or content is insufficient to judge (blurry, dark, too distant, ambiguous).
unrelated: image does not appear to show a civic problem.
unavailable: the analysis service failed (error, timeout, 503, malformed output). A service failure must never be presented as "unrelated".
Confidence labels: High / Medium / Low

No percentages. Derive the label from explicit, documented rules in code, not just from a number Gemini produces. Example rule set (adapt and document in code comments):

High: relevance = civic and description matches image and at least 2 specific visible observations and the suggested category is consistent with the (optional) ML text classifier's prediction.
Medium: relevance = civic but only one of the above is weak (for example description is vague, or classifier disagrees).
Low: relevance = unclear, or multiple signals conflict.

If the existing TF-IDF classifier is used as a cross-check, treat it as one signal, and show disagreement to administrators rather than hiding it.

Category selection
AI suggests; the citizen decides.
Pre-select nothing silently overwriting the citizen's own choice. If the citizen already chose a category, do not change it. Show "AI suggests X. Use this suggestion / Keep my choice."
Store aiSuggestedCategory (original, immutable) and citizenCategory (confirmed) separately, plus categoryOverriddenByCitizen.
Severity

AI may suggest low, medium or high and must explain why using visible evidence only. Do not invent injuries, population affected, contamination or risks that cannot be established from the image. The administrator's adminSeverity is the official value.

Explanation style

Plain language. Keep separate: Visible in the photo, AI assumptions, Needs human verification. Example: visible stagnant water does not prove harmful bacteria or mosquito breeding; say it "may" attract mosquitoes and needs inspection.

Recommendations
Suggested municipal action (inspection, repair type, extra verification).
Citizen guidance (safe actions, what to avoid, how to document).
Always state: "These are suggestions and do not confirm any official municipal action."
Unrelated image behaviour

Show a clear message: the image does not appear to show a civic problem; ask for a relevant photo. Offer a controlled manual-review override: the citizen writes why the photo is relevant (required, minimum length), the report is submitted with aiVerificationStatus = "unrelated", manualReviewRequested = true and the explanation stored. It is never shown or stored as AI-verified.

Unclear image behaviour

Offer: retake or upload a better photo, retry analysis, or submit for manual review (flagged unclear).

Service unavailable behaviour

Show an honest message ("Civic AI is temporarily unavailable"), offer retry and offer submission for manual review (flagged unavailable). Do not fabricate an analysis. Do not show cached results as fresh; if you ever show a previous result, label it with the time it was produced.

Reliability requirements
Server-side only: the Gemini key must never reach the browser. The frontend calls an authenticated Express endpoint (for example POST /api/ai/analyze).
Verify the caller's Firebase ID token on that endpoint; rate-limit per user.
Timeout (for example 30 s), retry with exponential backoff for 429, 500 and 503 (maximum 2 retries), safe handling of malformed JSON, empty responses, safety-blocked responses and network errors.
Image size and type limits before sending to Gemini (resize or compress server-side if needed).
Log technical detail on the server; return only a safe error code and friendly message to the client.
Do not claim Gemini works unless a live request has succeeded. Report the result of at least one real call, or state that you could not run one.
