9. POSSIBLE DUPLICATE AND RECURRING COMPLAINTS (DECISIONS ARE FINAL)
Purpose and boundaries
Administrator insight only.
Never show duplicate warnings to citizens.
Never auto-reject or auto-merge reports.
Preserve every original report and its history.
Admin decides whether it is a duplicate or a recurring issue.
Include both unresolved and previously resolved reports as comparison candidates, so admins can distinguish "same open problem reported again" from "recurrence at a location previously fixed".
No image similarity requirement. No complicated AI models. Use a transparent, explainable, unit-testable method.
Factors
Location: haversine distance between coordinates. Suggested default: related if within 50 m; "nearby" band up to 150 m with lower weight. Reports without coordinates are compared only by normalised address text and flagged as lower confidence.
Category: same category = full match; related categories (for example drainage and stagnant_water) = partial match, defined in a small explicit table.
Time: within 14 days of each other = strong; 15 to 60 days = moderate; older = weak. For previously resolved reports, measure time since resolvedAt to flag recurrence (for example reported again within 90 days of resolution).
Description: simple, explainable text similarity (lowercase, strip punctuation and stop words, Jaccard similarity or token-overlap on keywords; TF-IDF cosine is acceptable if already available). No external AI call.
Scoring and output
Keep thresholds and weights in one config object with comments so they can be tuned.
Return for each candidate: related report ID, category, location (and distance in metres), submission date, status, and a human-readable reason list (for example "42 m away", "same category", "reported 3 days apart", "descriptions share: pothole, bus stop").
Label the relationship type: "Possibly the same open issue" (related report unresolved) or "Possible recurrence" (related report previously resolved).
Run it on the server (or a pure shared function) when viewing report details and optionally when listing reports; avoid O(n²) work on every list render (use a bounding-box prefilter by coordinates, for example a geohash or lat/lng range query).
Show a compact "Possible related reports" section in the admin report details, plus a small indicator in the list. Ignore withdrawn and archived reports unless there is a reason to include them (document the decision).
Write unit tests (Section 16) for the matching function.
